# Ask Diego conventions

- The corpus is closed: bio, projects, credentials, education, and FAQ
  content that already ships elsewhere on the site. No open retrieval, no
  browsing, no filesystem access, no private data source, and no arbitrary
  visitor document.
- Every answer carries a citation into the corpus. When there is no
  sufficient match, return an explicit "don't know" response and never call
  a model provider to guess one.
- Keep answers near a 280-character guideline before citations; citations
  themselves do not count against that guideline.
- Send at most four prior messages of conversation context plus the current
  question and retrieved fragments — no unbounded history.
- The provider is optional, selected via `GROQ_MODEL`/`GROQ_API_KEY`, and
  off by default, including on Development or a local/unset `VERCEL_ENV`
  (only `VERCEL_ENV === "preview"` or `"production"` can activate it, and
  only once both vars are also set for that environment). Production
  activation was an explicit, separate authorization made only after a
  passing Preview verification (C9A, 2026-09-05) — it changed no model,
  limit, ZDR setting, corpus, or other control. With no provider
  configured, or on a `401`/`403`/`429`/`503`/timeout response, return
  `status: "fallback"` and point to the static FAQ; the rest of the site
  stays fully usable. The transport calls Groq's official OpenAI-compatible
  endpoint directly (`https://api.groq.com/openai/v1`), never a
  third-party gateway, and never retries a failed call.
- No tool call, function call, or side-effecting action is available to the
  model. It answers from retrieved text only.
- Never log a full prompt or a full model response. Log only bounded,
  allowlisted identifiers (status, latency, error category).
- Provider selection happens once at the server-side composition root; the
  endpoint code never branches on which provider is configured.
